-        "analysis": "'Why Your Credit Score Drops Even When You Pay On Time' is a 'Why Your X' explainer title built on a paradox: the viewer's responsible behavior (paying on time) is paired with an unexpected penalty (score drops). It names a concrete, personally owned object (your credit score) and a common frustration, so it is clearer than the channel's abstract mindset titles. It has no number, dollar figure or stakes marker (the script's strongest payoffs, the 22-point drop and the claimed $31,200 mortgage cost, are absent from the title), and it reads like a search-style FAQ rather than a surprising reveal. Same-channel comparisons: the closest topical sibling, 4eGrsL5CwBQ 'Why Everything Changes After You Get 832 Credit Score', published three days earlier, landed at 0.54383x TYPICAL, so two credit-score titles in one week both sat near the WEAK/TYPICAL boundary. Other 'Why Your [money object]' explainers are also low: J_sTOWazmho 'Why Your $1,000 Car Payment Actually Costs $4,000 (The Hidden Math)' 0.50669x TYPICAL despite a dollar figure; debt how-to r4fpD0dgQ64 'Fast Way to Pay Off $10K in Debt on a Small Salary' 0.2221x WEAK; gwm1Hr5iipU 'The 50 Year Mortgage Trap That'll Keep You in Debt Forever' 0.32481x WEAK. Counterexamples where 'Why Your' worked attach the question to a life-stage or wealth aspiration: mWhpJZrTyNU 'Why Your First Million Feels Impossible (And The Next Comes Fast)' 2.07599x STRONG and ZnI6pKMnpf4 'Why Your 40s Beat Your 20s for Building Wealth' 1.68072x TYPICAL; HEtVmp38D4M 'Invest or Pay Off Debt? The Wealthy Know the Answer' 3.64145x STRONG frames debt as a wealth decision; tynTfEi4518 'The Car Payment Trap Nobody Talks About' 24.51666x STRONG frames a debt object as a hidden trap. Within this small sample, credit/debt remediation framing co-occurs with weak results and aspiration or trap framing with stronger ones; this is descriptive across eight titles, not a proven cause.",
-        "search_demand": "Plausible explicit search intent ('why did my credit score drop', 'credit score dropped even though I paid on time' are natural queries), so the title is closer to search phrasing than most channel titles. Historical query volume, search traffic share and impressions are unavailable.",
-        "script_alignment": "Well aligned at the start: the cold open is exactly the promised scenario (bills paid in full, score falls 22 points from 738 to 716 on utilization). The script then broadens to seven traps (utilization snapshot, limit cuts, hard inquiries, closing old cards, paying off an installment loan, authorized-user late payments, score-version mismatch) plus report errors and a bureau business-model segment. Some sections (inquiries, report errors, multiple score versions) are not really 'drops while paying on time', so the title undersells the breadth and the Thomas mortgage-cost payoff."
+        "analysis": "The title uses a counterintuitive outcome—score decline despite on-time payment—to challenge the report-card mental model. It accurately signals the script's central point that payment history is only one input and that reported balances, limits, inquiries, account status, model choice and file errors can change a score. 'Drops' is not a guaranteed consequence of paying on time: the score can rise, fall or remain unchanged depending on the model and entire file. The script eventually says this, but its opening 22-point story makes a specific outcome feel more deterministic than the evidence permits. At 0.49765x against ten frozen same-channel age peers and 0.47287x against five, the result is WEAK in both cohorts; public metadata cannot establish whether the topic, phrasing or distribution caused that result.",
